hey there -

there are two issues here.    one is that we aren't catching 01000, will add that right now but unfortunately no release is planned for a few weeks, so instead of using a patch you can use the [documented event hook](https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/13/core/events.html?highlight=handle_error#sqlalchemy.events.ConnectionEvents.handle_error):

```
@event.listens_for(Engine, "handle_error")
def handle_exception(context):
    if isinstance(context.original_exception, pyodbc.Error):
        for code in (
            '01000', ):

            if code in str(context.original_exception):
                context.is_disconnect = True


```

second issue, your test is expecting pool_pre_ping to resume a transaction that was in progress when the database was stopped.  this behavior is not possible as documented at https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/13/core/pooling.html#pool-disconnects-pessimistic, so your test would need to be connecting on a new transaction in order for pre-ping to take effect.  existing transactions will definitely raise an error when the DB is stopped and additional SQL is emitted on those transactions.
